{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"When a U.S. ’40 Act ETF liquidates, shareholders normally receive their **pro rata share of the fund’s net assets**—typically cash after the portfolio is sold and liabilities and expenses are paid. That is not the same as losing the whole investment: you can still lose money if the assets are worth less than your purchase price, or if liquidation costs reduce proceeds. You generally do **not** receive your original investment or a guaranteed amount.\n\nThe fund announces its own liquidation and distribution schedule; there is no single timetable to assume. Trading may stop before the final distribution, and payment usually follows the portfolio’s wind-down and settlement.\n\nIn a taxable account, the liquidation is generally a taxable disposition: your gain or loss is measured against your tax basis, with capital-gain holding-period rules potentially applying. The fund may also distribute taxable income or capital gains during wind-down. Tax treatment and reporting can depend on the fund and your circumstances."}}
